    "body": "<p>This Signature primer designs a soundscape from three separate layer tracks, following the public edition&#x27;s rule that biophony, geophony, and anthrophony are stored separately and mixed only arithmetically. A Signature soundscape is specified by listing, for each layer, a level in decibels, a set of frequency bands, and a schedule of activity across the day, and the primer&#x27;s model combines those inputs into a total level, a band-occupancy table, and an NDSI-style balance figure. The primer is a teaching design: a student enters layer values, watches the balance move, and learns why a soundscape dominated by one layer sounds the way it does. Every input is invented, every output is labelled a Signature model, and no sound file is shipped or claimed to be a recording of a real place.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: the primer implements the published three-layer model and the published decibel-addition and NDSI arithmetic, applied to invented inputs. That combination, published formulas plus labelled invented inputs, is the standard form of a design model in soundscape teaching, and it is honest precisely because the formulas are public and the inputs are declared as designs.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: for layers at 55, 50, and 60 dB, the combined level is 10 x log10(10^5.5 + 10^5 + 10^6) = 61.51 dB. The demo prints the three layer levels, the combined level 61.51 dB, and the anthrophony power share, 10^6 / (10^5.5 + 10^5 + 10^6) = 70.6 percent.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony; geophony; anthrophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Signature design study for this database, October 2026; three-layer model and decibel arithmetic as in the public edition</p>",
